---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';
import TitleBlock from '../components/TitleBlock.astro';

const fields = [
  { k: 'Employer', v: 'City of Saskatoon' },
  { k: 'Department', v: 'Construction & Design' },
  { k: 'Role', v: 'Drafting Student' },
  { k: 'Terms', v: 'May–Aug 2025 · Jan–Apr 2026 · May–Aug 2026' },
  { k: 'Software', v: 'Civil 3D, AutoCAD, AutoLISP, C# .NET' },
];

const duties = [
  'Prepared and revised civil infrastructure drawings in Civil 3D, including water and sewer pipe drawings and road resurfacing plans.',
  'Updated permanent drawing records from as-built sketches and field data, to City drafting standards.',
  'Maintained maps, registered plans and easements as new information came in.',
  'Prepared work order documentation.',
  'Responded to public inquiries on behalf of the department.',
];
---
<Base
  title="Co-op work: City of Saskatoon"
  description="Three co-op work terms as a Drafting Student with the City of Saskatoon's Construction & Design Department: everyday civil drafting, and the AutoLISP and C# .NET tools I built to speed it up."
>
  <section class="work-head">
    <div class="wrap work-head__grid">
      <SectionHead as="h1" title="Co-op work at the City of Saskatoon">
        <p>
          Drafting Student in the Construction &amp; Design Department (Transportation &amp; Construction Division).
          Three work terms in 2025–2026. After my first term I was rehired for an 8-month term in 2026.
        </p>
      </SectionHead>
      <div data-reveal><TitleBlock fields={fields} /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section did" aria-labelledby="did">
    <SectionHead id="did" title="What I did" />
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <ul class="duties">
        {duties.map((d) => <li>{d}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p>
        This everyday drafting came first. I was asked to come back for an 8-month term in late July 2025,
        near the end of my first term, before I had built any tools.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tools-band" aria-labelledby="tools">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead id="tools" title="Tools I built">
        <p>Three tools for jobs I kept repeating at my desk. All three were built with AI assistance; how that works is further down.</p>
      </SectionHead>

      <article class="tool" aria-labelledby="t-autoslope" data-reveal>
        <header class="tool__head">
          <h3 id="t-autoslope">AutoSlope Labeller</h3>
          <p class="tool__kind">C# .NET plug-in (DLL) for Civil 3D</p>
        </header>
        <div class="tool__body">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              <strong>The problem.</strong> Construction drawings needed slope labels every 5 m along a road.
              By hand, each label means picking the alignment, the layer, the style and the one-way or 2-point method, over and over for the whole road.
            </p>
            <p><strong>What it does</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>Places slope labels every 5 m along a road alignment.</li>
              <li>Replaces picking the alignment, layer, style and method again for every single label.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="tool__result">
            <div class="bars" role="img" aria-label="Per 1 to 2 km road with labels every 5 m: 15 to 20 minutes by hand, 30 to 45 seconds with the tool.">
              <p class="bars__label">By hand · <span class="num">15–20 min</span></p>
              <div class="bar bar--before"><span style="--w:100%"></span></div>
              <p class="bars__label bars__label--after">With the tool · <span class="num">30–45 s</span></p>
              <div class="bar"><span style="--w:4%"></span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="bars" role="img" aria-label="Clicks per road: a couple hundred by hand, 10 to 15 with the tool.">
              <p class="bars__label">Clicks by hand · a couple hundred</p>
              <div class="bar bar--before"><span style="--w:100%"></span></div>
              <p class="bars__label bars__label--after">Clicks with the tool · <span class="num">10–15</span></p>
              <div class="bar"><span style="--w:6%"></span></div>
            </div>
            <p class="tool__note">Per 1–2 km road with labels every 5 m. Bars drawn to scale.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="story">
          <h4>How it started</h4>
          <ol class="timeline">
            <li>
              <span class="timeline__when">Feb–Mar 2026</span>
              <span>I was labelling whole roads every 5 m on construction drawings. I got curious: if the software is just code, can I build something to ease this?</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="timeline__when">Research</span>
              <span>I looked into how Civil 3D handles its data and which API I would need.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="timeline__when">Friday, late March 2026</span>
              <span>I went home with the idea and built it over the weekend, with AI assistance.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="timeline__when">Monday morning</span>
              <span>I showed it to the senior who had assigned me the drawings.</span>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="prose">Nobody asked me to build it. The seniors were impressed by how much time it saved.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="tool" aria-labelledby="t-candle" data-reveal>
        <header class="tool__head">
          <h3 id="t-candle">Candle</h3>
          <p class="tool__kind">AutoLISP routine</p>
        </header>
        <div class="tool__body">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              <strong>The problem.</strong> Drawing a manhole in profile by hand takes several steps: the lines, the right layer, style and lineweight, and the labels.
            </p>
            <p><strong>What it does</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>Click a point and enter its elevation, then a second point and its elevation.</li>
              <li>A third click draws the vertical “candle” for the manhole.</li>
              <li>Puts it on the correct layer, style and lineweight, and adds the labels.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="tool__result">
            <div class="bars" role="img" aria-label="Per manhole in profile: about 4 to 5 minutes by hand, under 30 seconds with the tool.">
              <p class="bars__label">By hand · <span class="num">~4–5 min</span></p>
              <div class="bar bar--before"><span style="--w:100%"></span></div>
              <p class="bars__label bars__label--after">With the tool · under <span class="num">30 s</span></p>
              <div class="bar"><span style="--w:11%"></span></div>
            </div>
            <p class="tool__note">Per manhole in profile. Bars drawn to scale.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="tool" aria-labelledby="t-pipe" data-reveal>
        <header class="tool__head">
          <h3 id="t-pipe">Pipe slope tool</h3>
          <p class="tool__kind">AutoLISP routine</p>
        </header>
        <div class="tool__body">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              <strong>The problem.</strong> Pipe slopes in profile were calculated by hand or copied, and each line was layered by hand.
            </p>
            <p><strong>What it does</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>Click a water main, sewer or storm pipe in profile.</li>
              <li>It detects which system the pipe belongs to from its layer.</li>
              <li>It calculates the slope and labels it as a percentage.</li>
              <li>It flags any line that isn’t on the standard layer.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <ul class="checks" aria-label="What one click does">
            <li><strong>Slope %</strong> calculated and labelled</li>
            <li><strong>System</strong> read from the layer: water main, sewer or storm</li>
            <li><strong>Layers</strong> checked, so standards are kept as I draft</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </article>

      <div class="prose more" data-reveal>
        <p>
          In June and July 2026 I kept building more AutoLISP routines whenever a task felt tedious.
          Some were adopted into the team’s workflow; others I kept for myself.
          After I presented a couple of them, the senior colleague who maintains the team’s Civil 3D standards
          recommended that I get VS Code and Microsoft Copilot access to build more, and I was given it in June 2026.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="how">
    <SectionHead id="how" title="How I build them" />
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <p>
        To be clear: the code in these tools is AI-assisted. I use Claude to plan, and an AI coding
        assistant to generate the AutoLISP or C#: at home for AutoSlope, and Microsoft Copilot in VS Code at work since June 2026. My part is the problem, the logic,
        finding the right API, testing and debugging in Civil 3D, and fitting the result to the City’s standards.
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Spot the repetitive task.</strong> I describe the idea to Claude, which helps me turn it into a roadmap.</li>
        <li><strong>Generate the code.</strong> An AI coding assistant (now Microsoft Copilot in VS Code) generates the LISP or C# from that plan.</li>
        <li><strong>Make it actually work.</strong> I find the right API, test it in Civil 3D (for example with VBADEBUG), debug it, and remove what isn’t needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Fit it to the City.</strong> I set it up for the City’s layers, styles and drafting standards.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="taught">
    <SectionHead id="taught" title="What co-op taught me" />
    <ul class="lessons">
      <li data-reveal data-reveal-group="lessons">
        <h3>Theory started to make sense.</h3>
        <p>What I had learned in class finally clicked once I was working on real drawings.</p>
      </li>
      <li data-reveal data-reveal-group="lessons">
        <h3>Reliability earns trust first.</h3>
        <p>Doing the everyday drafting carefully is what came first. Everything else came after.</p>
      </li>
      <li data-reveal data-reveal-group="lessons">
        <h3>AI is only as useful as your understanding.</h3>
        <p>The tools work because I understood the drafting task and the standards. Without that, the AI has nothing useful to build.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="note">Tool demos and City drawings aren’t shown here because they belong to the City of Saskatoon.</p>

    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn" href="/resume/">See my resume</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/projects/">School projects</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .work-head { padding-block: var(--space-6) var(--space-5); }
  .work-head__grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 7fr) minmax(0, 5fr); gap: var(--space-5); align-items: end; }
  .work-head__grid :global(.section-head) { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .duties { padding-left: 1.1em; }
  .duties li::marker { color: var(--accent); }

  .tools-band { background: var(--wall-2); padding-block: var(--space-6); }
  .tool { background: var(--plinth); border: 1px solid var(--line); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); box-shadow: 0 20px 50px rgba(20, 24, 40, 0.06); }
  .tool__head { padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4) 0; }
  .tool__head h3 { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-display); font-stretch: 112%; font-weight: 700; font-size: var(--step-2); letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  .tool__kind { margin: 0.35rem 0 0; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .tool__body { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4) var(--space-4); }
  .tool__result { align-self: start; display: grid; gap: var(--space-2); }
  .tool__result .bars { margin-top: 0; }
  .tool__note { margin: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .checks { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; align-self: start; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .checks li { padding: 0.9rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); margin: 0; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .checks strong { color: var(--accent); margin-right: 0.35rem; }

  .story { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4) var(--space-4); }
  .story h4 { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0; margin: 0; }
  .timeline { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: var(--space-2) 0; max-width: var(--measure); border-left: 1px solid var(--accent); }
  .timeline li { display: grid; gap: 0.15rem; padding: 0 0 var(--space-2) var(--space-3); position: relative; color: var(--ink-2); margin: 0; }
  .timeline li::before { content: ''; position: absolute; left: -4px; top: 0.5em; width: 7px; height: 7px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--accent); }
  .timeline__when { font-size: 0.85rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--accent); }

  .more { margin-top: var(--space-2); }

  .steps { padding-left: 1.6em; }
  .steps li::marker { font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--accent); }

  .lessons { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); display: grid; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .lessons li { margin: 0; padding-top: var(--space-2); border-top: 2px solid var(--ink); }
  .lessons h3 { margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  .lessons p { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .note { color: var(--ink-3); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .tool__body { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.3fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-5); }
    .lessons { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .work-head__grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-4); }
    .tool__head, .tool__body, .story { padding-inline: var(--space-2); }
  }
</style>
